Fix two crashes in the Julia GC integration

Backport of gap-system/gap#6729 to v4.16.1; drop it once GAP is updated
to v4.17.0.

This patch fixes two issues.

The first is that `MPtrMarkFunc` could mark the body of a bag after
Julia had freed it. It took the Julia type tag of the body as proof
that the body is still allocated. But Julia leaves the cells of a page
untouched when it frees the page as a whole, so a freed small bag can
keep its tag. A quick sweep can free the page of a dead body and skip
the page of its master pointer. If the conservative stack scan finds
that master pointer later, the freed body is queued for marking, and
since Julia 1.10 this crashes in `gc_setmark_pool_`, as a freed page
has no metadata. This is one way to get the backtrace collected in
oscar-system/GAP.jl#1224.

The second issue is that an old bag could leave the remembered set
while it still referenced a young object. The next quick collection
that did not reach the young object in another way then freed it. Mark
functions count the young objects a bag references, and `JMark`,
`JMarkTyped` and `MarkJuliaObjSafe` took the return value of
`jl_gc_mark_queue_obj` for "the object is young". Up to Julia 1.9 it
was. Since Julia 1.10 (JuliaLang/julia#47292) it tells whether the
call set the mark bit, which it does not if the object was marked
before. With several GC threads, another thread can mark a young bag
between the check in `MarkBag` and the call. With one GC thread, the
same happens to a young Julia object in an old bag, which `CLONE_OBJ`
can produce, if the collection reaches the object through another
reference first.

To fix these, we do the following:

- `MPtrMarkFunc` asks `jl_gc_internal_obj_base_ptr` whether the body
  of a small bag is allocated.
- The new helper `MarkQueueObj` reads from the GC bits whether an
  object is young. `JMarkTyped`, `MarkJuliaObj`, `MarkJuliaObjSafe` and
  `MPtrMarkFunc` report that. `JMark` reports nothing anymore, as the
  root scanners ignored its result.

The first fix does not cover large bags, as
`jl_gc_internal_obj_base_ptr` only knows pool objects, nor Julia
objects and Julia weak references held by a dead bag.

As an additional benefit, performance improves. Indeed, the check in
`MPtrMarkFunc` alone made a full collection 5% to 14% slower, but
`MPtrMarkFunc` also no longer reports an old body as young, which put
old master pointers into the remembered set for no reason. Together,
measured with GAP 4.16.1 and one GC thread:

Before:

    julia> using GAP, Chairmarks

    julia> l = GAP.evalstr("List([1..3000000], i -> [i])");

    julia> GC.gc(true); GC.gc(true)

    julia> @b GC.gc(true)
    313.477 ms (98.14% gc time, without a warmup)

After:

    julia> @b GC.gc(true)
    263.173 ms (99.98% gc time, without a warmup)

A quick collection that marks one million new lists took 60 to 65 ms
before and 34 to 36 ms after.

diff --git a/src/julia_gc.c b/src/julia_gc.c
index a960be47..487cda55 100644
--- a/src/julia_gc.c
+++ b/src/julia_gc.c
@@ -265,12 +265,26 @@ static void MarkAllSubBagsDefault(Bag bag, void * ref)
     MarkArrayOfBags(CONST_PTR_BAG(bag), SIZE_BAG(bag) / sizeof(Bag), ref);
 }
 
+// Queue `obj` for marking and return whether it is young. Mark functions
+// count the young objects a bag references, so that an old bag stays in
+// the remembered set. The return value of jl_gc_mark_queue_obj is of no
+// use for this: since Julia 1.10 it tells whether the call set the mark
+// bit, which it does not if `obj` was marked before, be it by another GC
+// thread.
+static inline int MarkQueueObj(jl_ptls_t ptls, void * obj)
+{
+    // 2 is GC_OLD
+    int young = !(jl_astaggedvalue(obj)->bits.gc & 2);
+    jl_gc_mark_queue_obj(ptls, (jl_value_t *)obj);
+    return young;
+}
+
 static inline int JMarkTyped(jl_ptls_t ptls, void * obj, jl_datatype_t * ty)
 {
     // only traverse objects internally used by GAP
     if (!jl_typeis(obj, ty))
         return 0;
-    return jl_gc_mark_queue_obj(ptls, (jl_value_t *)obj);
+    return MarkQueueObj(ptls, obj);
 }
 
 // Check that `obj` is still allocated and not on a free list already,
@@ -291,13 +305,13 @@ static inline int ValidTypeOfMarkedObj(void * obj)
     return jl_typeis(ty, jl_datatype_type);
 }
 
-static inline int JMark(jl_ptls_t ptls, void * obj)
+static inline void JMark(jl_ptls_t ptls, void * obj)
 {
 #ifdef VALIDATE_MARKING
     if (!ValidTypeOfMarkedObj(obj))
         abort();
 #endif
-    return jl_gc_mark_queue_obj(ptls, (jl_value_t *)obj);
+    jl_gc_mark_queue_obj(ptls, (jl_value_t *)obj);
 }
 
 // helper called directly by GAP.jl / JuliaInterface
@@ -307,7 +321,7 @@ void MarkJuliaObjSafe(void * obj, void * ref)
         return;
     if (!ValidTypeOfMarkedObj(obj))
         return;
-    if (jl_gc_mark_queue_obj(((MarkData *)ref)->ptls, (jl_value_t *)obj))
+    if (MarkQueueObj(((MarkData *)ref)->ptls, obj))
         ((MarkData *)ref)->youngRef++;
 }
 
@@ -316,7 +330,11 @@ void MarkJuliaObj(void * obj, void * ref)
 {
     if (!obj)
         return;
-    if (JMark(((MarkData *)ref)->ptls, obj))
+#ifdef VALIDATE_MARKING
+    if (!ValidTypeOfMarkedObj(obj))
+        abort();
+#endif
+    if (MarkQueueObj(((MarkData *)ref)->ptls, obj))
         ((MarkData *)ref)->youngRef++;
 }
 
@@ -684,7 +702,17 @@ static uintptr_t MPtrMarkFunc(jl_ptls_t ptls, jl_value_t * obj)
     void * ty = jl_typeof(header);
     if (ty != DatatypeSmallBag && ty != DatatypeLargeBag)
         return 0;
-    if (JMark(ptls, header))
+    // The tag alone does not prove that a small bag is still allocated:
+    // Julia leaves the cells of a page untouched when it frees the page as
+    // a whole.
+    if (ty == DatatypeSmallBag &&
+        jl_gc_internal_obj_base_ptr(header) != header)
+        return 0;
+#ifdef VALIDATE_MARKING
+    if (!ValidTypeOfMarkedObj(header))
+        abort();
+#endif
+    if (MarkQueueObj(ptls, header))
         return 1;
     return 0;
 }
